d within  the same one code base which of course  meant that if you change something on  the main behance website you had to go  and make sure that you didn't break it  on any of these other 40-plus versions  of the website no big deal so by now we  had a clear need for a tool that was  going to help us with our CSS  development not only we're vendor  prefixes starting to slip through the  cracks but thanks to all of these themed  galleries and websites we were  absolutely inundated with copy and  pasted CSS we're largely the differences  were things like font names or color  values  fortunately by this time CSS  preprocessors are really starting to  pick up steam and they were looking like  the perfect tools to help us with our  CSS in the shape and make things a bit  more dry now the story I'm about to tell  is not about the merits of any one CSS  preprocessor over the other but I do  want to use it as a real-world example  of the messy path to choosing  implementing and using new tools so for  us we were debating between less and  sass both excellent tools on their own  but we wanted to proper due diligence  and make sure that we picked the right  tool for our specific needs ultimately  sass had a more compelling  implementations and features for our use  case and compass on top of that offered  us a ton of extra power that was going  to be absolutely perfect for theming all  of those sites and for our sprite  management I mean sass and compass were  seriously set to improve our lives  drastically the answer for us could not  have been more clear we obviously went  with less why would we do that in our  case we ended up going with less for a  couple of reasons at the time there was  only Ruby sass and since Behance was not  built on a Ruby stack it wasn't easy for  us easy for us to implement or even  start using sass and it was a little bit  intimidating since less could easily be  compiled by just downloading a  convenient little GUI app to your  computer it actually is a really low  barrier to entry for us we were still a  very tiny dev team at this point but  this meant that we could start getting a  lot of the benefits of a CSS  preprocessor much sooner and with way  less friction and engineering time than  if we had chosen to go with sass at that  point it afforded us another interesting  benefit as well in that we were actually  able to use less as a proof of concept  that implementing a CSS preprocessor was  a worthwhile investment the time it took  to get it up and running for everyone to  use paid off drastically in development  time and development improvements down  the line which meant that we were able  to get more to  by in for actually adopting SAS as well  as negotiate for the development time  that we needed to do so and make the  switch to the tool that we ultimately  felt would serve us best but our journey  does not end there  although we had decided that the higher  investment in setting up SAS was worth  it we did have to cut some corners to  get there to complicate things we were  now dealing with the dreaded technical  debt of working in a code base that had  partially vanilla CSS and partially less  syntax and now we would have to spend  the time to convert that all into SAS  and we still didn't have a tech stack  that could compile SAS for us but what  we could do was install Ruby and SAS on  each of our individual computers  and compile and commit the SAS and CSS  to our repositories from there now we  violated a lot of web development best  practices by compiling CSS on our own  machines instead of by using a build  tool within the tech stack and we were  committing an even worse crime of  actually committing compiled code to our  repository I seriously doubt you to ever  find a medium think piece advocating for  this kind of implementation and it gives  me some anxiety to admit on stage in  front of you all that we were committing  such an egregious violation of best  practices by having literally hundreds  of compiled CSS files in our repository  but on the flip side I also cannot  overstate how much benefit we were  getting by having SAS available to us in  this way for what sounds like a  nightmare situation on the surface I  have to say for the most part actually  worked really well for us suddenly we  were able to build our themed galleries  and white labeled websites in a fraction  of the time which meant that we were  helping our users in our mission to give  more exposure to creatives we were able  to do it with way less code than before  which meant that now our code base  became much easier to understand and  maintain  although committing compiled code is  considered a bad practice for a large  complex code base with a lot of  interdependencies it actually ended up  giving us extremely clear visibility  into the reach that any single change  actually was having and the last cool  thing is that we suddenly had tools that  could do some grunt work for us  for example this meant that our  designers could focus on working on and  designing things that actually mattered  rather than spending their time doing  menial tasks like stitching sprites  together so in this case we were able to  find a balance between a subpar  technical implementation and  overwhelming developer and designer  happiness as well as code health by  reducing a lot of the technical debt in  our CSS but what happens when you need a  tool that doesn't really seem to exist  yet around this time we started working  on a new product it was a single page  web app for task management we called  action method online being that it was a  completely brand new product and totally  different from behance  we were starting from scratch and we had  an excellent and rare opportunity to try  out new technologies and we got to do  this without having to worry about  retrofitting it into any existing code  now I think we chose less for this one  because of the timing but on the  JavaScript side we