ebsite responsive but despite  the challenges in place by the existing  foundation we knew a full rewrite of the  website was simply out of the question  it was a huge website and it would have  taken far too long so we continued  working on Behance we would add new  features and convert the site over to be  responsive page by page and we used our  JavaScript framework NBD for any new  code that we were creating and then we  would update any existing areas of the  site to use it as needed also whether it  was because that page was due for  maintenance or any other kind of update  so both sass and NBD carried us all the  way into 2015 when we  started work on another brand new single  page web app this time it was a website  builder called Adobe portfolio and it  stood to be the most complex single page  web app that we've created to date we  had a ton of new technical challenges of  portfolio that we had never had to deal  with before even in our previous single  page web app what we wanted was build a  full GUI what you see is what you get  editor where by using one of any  literally hundreds of customization  dialogs you can make change to your  website and see it reflected in the  editor in real time we needed to do this  all while maintaining an editor chrome  that needed to not interfere or  intersect with user customizations in  any way so we started building portfolio  with NBD but building and maintaining  framework is not without its risks and  downsides either and by now we've been  starting to feel some of the growing  pains of actually using our own  homegrown framework I was originally  built by just one of our developers and  maintained by only a couple more so this  meant that it didn't move particularly  fast meant that other open source  frameworks were moving at a much faster  rate it also meant that it was much more  challenging for new hires to learn it  documentation on it was sparse because  the developers who knew how it worked  were busy developing whether it was the  framework or for new features for our  websites and then also the universe of  resources to get help with it was scoped  entirely to an extremely small portion  of our dev team which then unfortunately  made it hard for anyone who needed help  to be self-sufficient on the flip side  popular open source frameworks came with  the benefit of way more active invisible  communities to tap into for help which  not only means you have a way bigger  pool of resources to get help from but  it also means that there is a community  need that you can give back to when you  solve a specific challenge or  interesting problem yourself so by then  it was easy enough to acknowledge that  our homegoing framework was no longer  really the best tool for our team or  new types of products and features that  we were building after spending some  time on portfolios development we turned  our attention back to Behance and in the  time that we had been building adobe  portfolio our designers had been working  on designing a whole new look and feel  for the entire Behance website and we  wanted this to make a big splash we  wanted it to be a release where the look  and feel of the entire up site would  change for our users all at once and  despite this once again we still knew  that starting the whole thing over from  scratch was simply not an option for us  we'd adopted modular JavaScript with  requirejs and attempted to scope and  componentize our CSS as much as possible  using FEM but the truly Scopes nature of  CSS and j/s was becoming impossibly  enticing for our increasingly modular  web sites and web apps so with a big  overhaul on our plates the time was once  again ripe for us to look for new  technology now in the same way that the  industry at large has a lot of opinions  on the best tools to use so does any  individual given dev team so what we did  to figure out what was gonna work best  for us is we would hold group  discussions lunch and learns different  knowledge sharing sessions for anyone  who is interested where we would discuss  the pros and cons and the merits of  different jobs for frameworks or CSS and  J's solutions and then when we could get  some kind of agreement on which one's  seen the most compelling we would  actually try them out by building parts  of the website for them specifically our  at the admin section of our websites  which were just pages that only  employees can access for different  maintenance tasks but what it meant was  that we could actually start using these  things in a near real-world proving  ground  so for Behance we ultimately decided on  view and CSS modules as our preferred  tools and once again we continue to use  a lot of the same techniques that we for  implementing new technology that we had  done in the past who we exclusively  built new features using these new  and frameworks which meant that they got  to be in use immediately we rebuilt  existing UI components such as buttons  and inputs using these new tools and  frameworks alongside their existing  implementations which could then be  moved over to different areas of the  site as those pages were updated to use  view as well and perhaps most critically  we did all of this development behind  what we call feature flags what this is  is just a check in the code that serves  one set of code or another depending on  your users gatekeeper setting which  means that we could build everything  that we needed alongside the existing  website without worrying about  interfering with it while also still  having the ability to see all of this  new stuff in action in a production  environment even before it was ready for  its public debut and so this actually  brings us to where we are today the  website is not fully converted to view  and CSS and j/s but we continue to  follow the same practices as we update  and develop new features and actually as  an aside although we are fairly recently  into our transition into view w